Z||000000||Slammed Fiero||01-26-2001||10:30 AM||||I potentially have a 3.1ltr motor froma friend. No heads , just a long block assembly. I would like to rebuild it and replace my 2.8. What is the largest it can be bored and stroked?? 3.2??<P>any suggestions for performance parts??<BR>There used to be an HP book out there on how to rebuild a 60 degree V6 , is it still in print?<P>Thanks<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Shaun41178||01-26-2001||01:05 PM||||Hey Johnny boy!  Yep 3.2 is as large as it is going to get.  get a 3.1 crank and matching pistons and a neutral flexplate/flywheel and then have it all balanced.<P>If you get an aluminum block it can get out to 3.3 liters.  Want an aluminum block?  I have one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>That book is still in print and can be found at the Fiero Store for one and probably Barnes and Nobles too.<P>how much hp you want?  i recommend a cam from gm.  427 intake 454 exhaust lift with 204/216 duration.  price of cam and lifter kit is only around $130 US  Get some hyperhuetectic pistons or go with forged.  thinking of forced induction then go with a set of quality rings.  If not then just go with a set of stock rings.  Just some sugestions<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eric||01-26-2001||04:15 PM||||First off, find a machinist/ engine builder in your area.  If you already have the short block, you could just plug your 2.8 heads on it, maybe throw in that big cam Shaun was talking about (really it's about the cheapest cam available for our motors.  I paid like 115 through the local GM hipo dealership.).  If you would do all of us a favor when you take the heads off this 3.1.  Please tell us if there's a dish on the stock pistons.  I guess we also need to know if it was an iron head engine or aluminum.  We're trying to get some idea of the compression ratio of these built 3.2 motors.  The hypereutectic replacements are dished slightly.<P>I personally have had some tuning problems with my 3.2.  Until recently, when I changed out the mangled orginal ypipe, the car made no upper rpm power.  Now it's breathing a little more freely, but we're still not quite there.  I still need to take out the old clogged up cat and there's a pinch in the exaust pipe down the line a little.  I'd also like to get the fancy crane coil, ported throttle body and maybe a custom burned chip programed for bigger injectors.  I've got some 0-60 times through a Gtech of low to mid seven second runs.  This is an improvement of almost a full second before I changed the ypipe.  The engine is still very strong down in the lower rpm range, not much out there short of 4wd can beat if of the line, much less to 50 or so.  It doesn't have much go above 85 or 90, much like the stock motor.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||01-26-2001||05:15 PM||||Don't bore the motor to its maximum bore of .060". You will only gain about 1-3 horsepower and you could end up with less power if the block flexes too much because you removed too much meat from the cylinders. You should bore it as little as possible especially if your going to add a turbo or nitrous.<P>The GM Performance 12363220 camshaft is an excellent camshaft for this motor because it gives you both good low-end torque and upper end horsepower.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT GT 87||01-26-2001||06:34 PM||||As far as bored and stroked 2.8s go .RSM in Canada , has a great sounding 3.3 W/9.2  compresion,stock heads and cam(GT)long block 175 Hp for 1500$  Join their club and get 15% off no charge for the club! They want to bore more but 1.6 rockers and headers ( they sell) should be pretty cool. When my IRS tax check comes in , I'm gunnna do it.            STEVE<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Slammed Fiero||01-26-2001||07:07 PM||||I plan to run a turbo set up using a T3 Unit, with Ross Forged racing pistons 8.0 : 1.0 compression. block will be bored slightly to clean up existing bores , de- burred. rotating assembly balnced ect..ect.<P>Has anyone looked into getting their crank knife edged??? We had it done when we built our 4.5ltr all aluminum V8 for a corvair , lightened the rotating assembly quite a bit , <P><BR> <BR>    <P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||01-27-2001||12:38 PM||||hey johnathan, is that the corvair convertible with the mid mounted v8? If so I saw it at Duncan Falls car show last summer. Pretty wild!!<P>That the turbo fun, mine isnt fast off the line but boy when it winds up waaaaaaahooooo<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>I plan to run a turbo set up using a T3 Unit, with Ross Forged racing pistons 8.0 : 1.0 compression. block will be bored slightly to clean up existing bores , de- burred. rotating assembly balnced ect..ect.<P>Has anyone looked into getting their crank knife edged??? We had it done when we built our 4.5ltr all aluminum V8 for a corvair , lightened the rotating assembly quite a bit , <P><BR> <BR>    <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||01-27-2001||04:05 PM||||Actually, the "How to Rebuild you GM 60* V6" went out of print a few months ago.  I got lucky and found one sitting at Barnes & Noble, the Fiero Store probably has a few copys.  It's a good book, nothing Fiero specific but definatly usefull.||||reg||
Z||000008||Slammed Fiero||01-27-2001||05:21 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://home.ican.net/~blur/v8.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://home.ican.net/~blur/v8e.gif"> <P><BR>This was before the rebuild. The car runs low 13's with 3.23 rear gears. 6 link rear suspension  , no back seat , lowered , Koni's ..96.56 points in the concour at the Corvair national convention.. The motor runs backwards (like a honda) from the conventional rotation of an engine sice the engine sits behind and not infront of the transmission/ Gear box. Corbeau Gt8 seats , revs smoothly to 7500rpm<P>fun fun fun ..Too bad I never get to drive it..I get in enough trouble with a 2.8<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||01-27-2001||07:24 PM||||You can't reduce the weight of the crankshaft anymore than the weight of the reciprocating assembly (piston, rod, and rings) or you won't be able to balance the engine. The best way to reduce the weight of your reciprocating assembly is to buy very light high quality forged pistons and have the crankshaft balanced. You can also buy an aluminum flywheel if you have a manual transmission. <P>The pistons I am talking about would have cost me almost $1000 for my 3.4-liter engine.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-27-2001).]||||reg||1
